    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2027 Suzuki SV-7S Unveiled at the EICMA Show in Italy: I’m Suddenly More Nostalgic Than Ever\nMaserati To Unveil Concepts Previewing the Next Levante and Quattroporte in December 2026\n14 photos\nPhoto: Maserati\nDecember 10, 2026. That's when the House of the Trident will purportedly debut a couple of concepts that are supposed to preview the next-generation Levante and Quattroporte.\nThis information comes from The Supercar Blog rather than Maserati. Neither concept has received official confirmation from the Modenese automaker. Be that as it may, we do know for certain that Stellantis gave its blessing to Maserati for two larger models.\nAs far as the Levante's replacement is concerned, everyone and their dog knows that Maserati needs a larger sport utility vehicle in the lineup. The Grecale simply does not cut it alone, especially in markets where bigger is better. Just like its predecessor, the new Levante will have to prove its mettle against BMW's X5, the Mercedes GLE, and the Porsche Cayenne.\nThe Quattroporte, meanwhile, would have to be less conventional than just another performance-oriented luxury sedan. Porsche already knows that such vehicles don't sell too well, but nevertheless, the German automaker is currently prepping a mid-cycle refresh of the Panamera.\nDuring its FaSTLAne 2030 strategy announcement on May 21, 2026, the multinational automotive group confirmed that Maserati has two new E-segment vehicles in the offing. Technically speaking, E-segment means E-Class and 5 Series rather than a full-size luxobarge like the S-Class and 7 Series.\nPhoto: Maserati The group's announcement failed to identify either vehicle by name or disclose production dates. Given the aforementioned, the concepts reported by The Supercar Blog should not be treated as hearsay. If you ask me, the next Quattroporte deserves particular attention because of Santo Ficili.\nMaserati's chief executive officer made it clear that a traditional sedan profile does not cut it, describing a low-slung vehicle that blurs the line between a shooting brake and a crossover. A rather unusual direction for the Quattroporte, but not entirely surprising. Toyota did it as well, replacing the sedan-bodied Avalon with the fastback-style Crown.\nIn any case, a Quattroporte with different proportions from its forerunners would attract new customers for the brand. The practicality of a larger cargo area would also make it more attractive. Combined with a higher seating position and some crossover touches here and there, Maserati could be onto something here.\nThat approach is a lot more interesting than simply responding to the S-Class and 7 Series, and Maserati has always had an easier time attracting attention when it does something different. The Levante has less room for experimentation, though, because mid-size luxury sport utility vehicles are very hot as of this writing.\nBack in January 2024, the company outlined a product strategy built around electric-only successors for the Levante and Quattroporte, models that would have debuted in 2027 and 2028 . Considering that Maserati canceled the development of the MC20 Folgore in March 2025, chances are the new Levante and Quattroporte will offer internal combustion and hybrid options.\nVia // The Supercar Blog\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Maserati Levante Maserati Levante maserati 2028 Maserati Levante 2027 Maserati Quattroporte Maserati Quattroporte concept 2028 Maserati Quattroporte\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT November Mini GT Preorder Reveals Six Fast Cars: One of Them Is a New Casting!",
